   "text": {
    "L1": "Burning is a chemical change: a fuel reacts with oxygen and gives out heat and usually light.",
    "L2": "A fire needs three things: fuel, oxygen from air, and enough heat to reach its ignition temperature.",
    "L3": "Our sigri coals are fuel and are already hot enough, but they are short of oxygen.",
    "L4": "Fanning pushes fresh, oxygen-rich air onto the coals, so combustion becomes faster and gives more heat and light.",
    "L5": "Covering a flame cuts off oxygen, so the fire goes out.",
    "L6": "Burning does not destroy matter. Wood, wax or oil become gases, smoke and ash.",
    "L7": "Rusting is also a chemical change that needs oxygen, but it is slow, needs water and gives no flame.",
    "L8": "Your turn: which one of these is needed for burning?"
   },
